      "id": 3,
      "text": "John’s Episcopal Church in Warehouse Point earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places for its architectural significance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Windsor%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "William Pynchon built a warehouse at what is now Warehouse Point in 1636, making it a key transshipment hub on the Connecticut River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Windsor%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "East Windsor lies within Hartford County and is part of the Capitol Planning Region, linking it to the state’s political core.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Windsor%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town is divided into five villages—Broad Brook, Melrose, Scantic, Warehouse Point, and Windsorville—each with its own historic charm.",
